<p>There are four states in which matter can exist: solid, liquid, gas, and plasma. Similarly, in an April Fools’ Day paper in 2022, Harvard&#8217;s Madelyn Leembruggen and Caroline Martin proposed that foods can be classified as soup, salad, or sandwich:</p>
<ul>
<li>&#8220;Soups are a suspension of ingredients in a liquid matrix, whereas salads are a suspension of ingredients in a gaseous matrix.&#8221; Thus cereal is soup, and mixed nuts are salad. The transition between phases is continuous: Depending on how finely they&#8217;re chopped, the same set of ingredients might produce a chunky salad, a salsa, or a gazpacho. Vanilla ice cream is a &#8220;glassy&#8221; soup because it doesn&#8217;t flow at its serving temperature, but it takes on a more liquid character as it melts.</li>
<li>A soup or salad becomes a sandwich when it&#8217;s enclosed in a carbohydrate. The shape and quality of the carbohydrate must vary according to the substance it encloses, but a proposed general rule is that if you can pick up a dish by the carb and eat it, it&#8217;s a sandwich.</li>
</ul>
<p>Further investigation is needed into some edge cases (e.g., lasagna vs. baked ziti), but in most cases a lot of ground can be covered by just asking &#8220;Is this a soup, a salad, or a sandwich?&#8221; &#8220;Most of the time, the answer you blurt out with the least amount of thought is the most true.&#8221;</p>
<p>&#8220;Finally, to remove any of the reader&#8217;s lingering uncertainty: yes, a hotdog is a sandwich.&#8221;</p>
<p>(Madelyn Leembruggen and Caroline Martin, <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2203.16580">&#8220;What&#8217;s for Lunch? A Systematic Ordering of Foods in the Soup-Salad-Sandwich Phase Space,&#8221;</a> arXiv preprint arXiv:2203.16580 [2022].)</p>
